<p>There are two <a href=3D"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/l=
ive-delegation">delegation modes</a>:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>Responses delegation.</strong> An OpenAI backend model gets t=
he conversation context through the Responses API.</li>
  <li><strong>Client delegation.</strong> GPT-Live emits a delegation event=
 and your code handles it, so you can route to your own agents.</li>
</ol>

<p>Client delegation in particular is very powerful: it lets you add full-d=
uplex behavior on top of an existing system of agents. Kwindla: =E2=80=9CWe=
 can now build things like state machine conversation graphs, threaded/back=
tracking modes, and structured data input that were difficult with the firs=
t generation of speech-to-speech APIs.=E2=80=9D</p>

<p>This kind of frontend-backend separation is pretty much accepted as the =
correct way of building voice agents.<span class=3D"sn"><button class=3D"sn=
-ref" aria-label=3D"guess">=E2=9C=A6</button><span class=3D"sn-body" style=
=3D""><span class=3D"sn-tag">guess</span>One could imagine the frontend bei=
ng small enough to run on-device, talking to a large server-side backend.</=
span></span> What is new is that the behavior is trained into the model. Kw=
indla again: =E2=80=9CIt=E2=80=99s great to see this pattern trained into a=
 production model, so that we can leverage it natively at the prompt level.=
=E2=80=9D</p>

<p>Details from the docs:</p>

<ul>
  <li>In client delegation, the frontend sends no task description to the b=
ackend. From the <a href=3D"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/l=
ive-delegation">delegation guide</a>: =E2=80=9CThe delegation object contai=
ns metadata, not task text.=E2=80=9D You collect user and agent transcript =
deltas, and the backend infers the task from them. =E2=80=9CA transcript fr=
agment is not a complete user turn, and transcripts may contain mistakes.=
=E2=80=9D</li>
  <li>Backend results go into the frontend=E2=80=99s context in <a href=3D"=
https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/live-delegation">three ways</=
a>: <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">session.instructio=
ns.append</code> (=E2=80=9CStop speaking about that request.=E2=80=9D), <co=
de class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">session.commentary.append=
</code> for things to be spoken (=E2=80=9CYour appointment is confirmed for=
 Thursday at 2:00 PM=E2=80=9D), and <code class=3D"language-plaintext highl=
ighter-rouge">session.thinking.append</code> for internal context (=E2=80=
=9CChecking Thursday availability. No appointment has been booked.=E2=80=9D=
).</li>
  <li>Each append is <a href=3D"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guid=
es/live-delegation">limited to 500 tokens</a>. The frontend acknowledges ea=
ch one with a corresponding <code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-r=
ouge">*.appended</code> event.</li>
  <li>The frontend verbalizes structured results itself. From the <a href=
=3D"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/live-delegation">delegati=
on guide</a>: =E2=80=9CA concise tool result doesn=E2=80=99t need an additi=
onal model call to rewrite it for speech.=E2=80=9D</li>
  <li>Images go to a =E2=80=9Cvision-capable backend=E2=80=9D, and typed ex=
act values such as an order number go =E2=80=9Cto the backend that handles =
the task=E2=80=9D (<a href=3D"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides=
/live-delegation">delegation guide</a>). UI context is passed to the fronte=
nd only as a concise text summary of the current page. The frontend itself =
only handles voice and text.</li>
</ul>

<h3 id=3D"training-for-delegation">Training for delegation</h3>

<p>The frontend has to learn three things:<span class=3D"sn"><button class=
=3D"sn-ref" aria-label=3D"guess">=E2=9C=A6</button><span class=3D"sn-body" =
style=3D""><span class=3D"sn-tag">guess</span>How would you train this? In =
SFT, with sandboxed backends and scripted responses. In RL, with environmen=
ts containing real or mocked backends, which is expensive.</span></span></p=
>

<ol>
  <li>When to delegate a task to the backend</li>
  <li>How to carry on the conversation while the task runs asynchronously</=
li>
  <li>How to gracefully incorporate the backend response</li>
</ol>

<blockquote class=3D"oq">
  <p><strong>Open question:</strong> What did the training setup for delega=
tion look like? In particular, how were asynchronous backends, with realist=
ic latency and occasional bad responses, simulated at RL scale? This is the=
 most valuable thing to know for anyone trying to build a comparable model.=
</p>
</blockquote>

<h3 id=3D"prompting-the-frontend">Prompting the frontend</h3>

<p>The <a href=3D"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/live-prompt=
ing">prompting guide</a> is mostly about turn-taking behavior. Through the =
frontend prompt you control response length, language and pronunciation of =
names (via IPA), interpreter mode, when to respond (e.g. only to certain ki=
nds of queries), handling of background noise, and asking for clarification=
s.</p>

<h2 id=3D"what-the-frontend-models">What the frontend models</h2>

<p><strong>Audio tokens directly.</strong> You can =E2=80=9Cshape an agent=
=E2=80=99s tone, pace, and conversational style through the system prompt,=
=E2=80=9D e.g. =E2=80=9CSpeak warmly and naturally, at an unhurried pace.=
=E2=80=9D You cannot change the voice. There are 12 fixed voices (Quartz, R=
ipple, Vesper, Willow, Stone, Gleam, Meridian, Bossa, Tempo, Beacon, Delta,=
 Cinder), disjoint from the 10 Realtime voices. This is consistent with a s=
ingle model generating acoustic tokens rather than a TTS stage.<span class=
=3D"sn"><button class=3D"sn-ref" aria-label=3D"guess">=E2=9C=A6</button><sp=
an class=3D"sn-body" style=3D""><span class=3D"sn-tag">guess</span>The prom=
otion and evals focus on delegation and turn-taking, so most post-training =
effort probably went into the frontend=E2=80=99s conversational behavior, w=
ith the audio encoder and vocoder kept close to GPT-Realtime=E2=80=99s. The=
 voice sets differ, so the vocoder was at least retrained. <a href=3D"https=
://x.com/kundan2510/status/2098149959073935711">Kundan Kumar</a> from OpenA=
I: =E2=80=9Cwe did specific training to further improve the controllability=
 and instruction following of the model.=E2=80=9D</span></span></p>

<blockquote class=3D"oq">
  <p><strong>Open question:</strong> Did the audio encoder and vocoder chan=
ge from GPT-Realtime? The completely new voice set says the vocoder was at =
least retrained. Nothing public says anything about the encoder.</p>
</blockquote>

<p><strong>User and agent text.</strong> GPT-Live-1 =E2=80=9Cnatively provi=
des ASR transcripts and response text=E2=80=9D and =E2=80=9Csupports keywor=
d biasing.=E2=80=9D The model predicts user text as it hears audio, and gen=
erates agent text before agent audio. <a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510=
.07497">We did the same for Moshi in our paper</a>, which let us match S2S =
intelligence with T2T.</p>

<p><strong>A reasoning trace.</strong> The backend can inject text through =
<code class=3D"language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">session.thinking.appen=
d</code>, which is described as context for the model=E2=80=99s internal re=
asoning. <strong>This means the frontend also maintains a reasoning trace, =
in addition to the user and agent audio streams and the user and agent text=
 streams.</strong></p>

<p><strong>Turn tokens.</strong> =E2=80=9CAlthough GPT-Live-1 is not a turn=
-based model, it natively supports turn detection.=E2=80=9D So it is traine=
d with special tokens for user turn start and end.</p>

<p>Putting this together, Figure 2 shows what the frontend=E2=80=99s sequen=
ce might look like, including a delegation. We have drawn the streams as se=
parate rows for clarity, but the model would put them all in a single inter=
leaved sequence, with special tokens to demarcate the boundaries. Note that=
 the frontend keeps consuming user audio in chunks throughout, including wh=
ile the backend is working.</p>

