<h2 id=3D"latency-and-chunking">Latency and chunking</h2>

<p>Turn-taking latency on <a href=3D"https://github.com/DanielLin94144/Full=
-Duplex-Bench/tree/main/v1_v1.5/dataset">Full-Duplex-Bench v1</a> is 0.8 s.=
 Moshi gets 0.25 s and PersonaPlex under 0.2 s. But those are multi-stream =
models operating on an 80 ms clock (see <a href=3D"https://x.com/rdesh26/ar=
ticle/2054954720024801280">my earlier article</a> on multi-stream v/s inter=
leaved models). This suggests that GPT-Live-1 is operating on a larger cloc=
k.<span class=3D"sn"><button class=3D"sn-ref" aria-label=3D"guess">=E2=9C=
=A6</button><span class=3D"sn-body" style=3D""><span class=3D"sn-tag">guess=
</span>Most likely it still uses 80 ms tokens, but prefills user audio in c=
hunks of 2 to 5 tokens (160 to 400 ms) and decodes between chunks. TML uses=
 200 ms chunks, for comparison.</span></span></p>

<p>Two things that are <em>not</em> evidence for the chunk size:</p>

<ul>
  <li>The <a href=3D"https://developers.openai.com/cookbook/examples/audio/=
voice_agent_evaluation">eval harness</a> streams audio in 20 ms PCM appends=
. That is a transport choice. Audio is buffered on both sides.</li>
  <li>The RUN harness =E2=80=9Cbuilds a 400-millisecond playback reserve.=
=E2=80=9D In the code, this is a jitter buffer on the output side: GPT-Live=
=E2=80=99s output audio deltas have no timestamps, so the relay holds up to=
 400 ms before forwarding. It says something about how bursty the output is=
, not about input chunking.</li>
</ul>

<blockquote class=3D"oq">
  <p><strong>Open question:</strong> What are the actual frame rate and chu=
nk size? The 0.8 s turn-taking latency and the per-minute pricing both fit =
chunked prefill on an 80 ms clock, but a coarser tokenizer would fit too. A=
 direct answer would settle most of the guesses in this post.</p>
</blockquote>

<h2 id=3D"can-we-infer-model-size-from-token-pricing">Can we infer model si=
ze from token pricing?</h2>

<p>GPT-Live-1 costs $0.05 per minute, billed per second. The backend is bil=
led separately, and rate limits are per concurrent session rather than per =
token. There is no per-token price for the frontend, but we can try to back=
 one out. Some assumptions:</p>

<ul>
  <li>The audio tokenizer runs at 12.5 Hz (80 ms frames), which is the most=
 common rate for full-duplex models. The user and agent streams then produc=
e 750 tokens per minute each.</li>
  <li>User audio is prefilled in chunks of 5 tokens, so one minute needs 15=
0 prefills. Agent audio is decoded.</li>
  <li>Text (user transcript, thinking, agent text) adds about 1 token per 5=
 audio tokens, i.e. ~300 decoded tokens per minute.</li>
</ul>

<p>That gives 750 prefilled and ~1000 decoded tokens per minute. Let us mod=
el the token pricing under these assumptions and see which GPT model would =
charge $0.05 for that mix. The sliders change the assumptions.</p>

